## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also removed (`SYSLIB0006`).

### The Fundamental Shift

**.NET Framework:** Runtime forcibly injects `ThreadAbortException` (preemptive).
**.NET 8:** Code must **voluntarily check** for cancellation (cooperative).

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout; throws on cancellation
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

If your callers can go async (recommended):

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken externalToken = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(externalToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(work, cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (externalToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw; // Caller cancelled — propagate as-is
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| **Work ignores the token** | If `Func<T> work` is a long CPU loop that never checks a `CancellationToken`, cancellation won't interrupt it — the task keeps running silently. Pass `CancellationToken` into the work: `Func<CancellationToken, T>`. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | `CTS` is `IDisposable`; always wrap in `using`. Leaking it leaks internal timers. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Distinguish timeout vs. caller cancellation using `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` guards. |
| **Expecting immediate stop** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cooperative cancellation only takes effect at checkpoints (`token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()`). Design work to check frequently. |

### If Work Can't Cooperate

If `Func<T>` calls third-party/native code you can't modify to accept a token, **there is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort`**. Move the work to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` on timeout:

```csharp
// Last resort for uncooperative code
var process = Process.Start("worker.exe", args);
if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException("Worker process timed out");
}
```

### What Was Removed and Why

| Removed API | Replacement |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` to decide whether to continue |
